/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A signal is a function of an independent variable that carries information.
   - A continuous-time (CT) signal has a value for every real t in its range.
   - A discrete-time (DT) signal has a value only at integer n.
   - x(t) is the CT signal; Ts is the sampling interval in seconds; n is the integer index; x[n] is the resulting sequence.
   - A system is an operator that turns an input signal into an output signal.
   - Treat the system as a black box.
   - Two examples fix the idea.
   - Discrete-time signals are not always sampled.
   - The two families need different tools.
   - Answer. x(t) is a CT signal and x[n] is a DT signal; x(0.025 s) = 4 V and x[5] = 0 V.
*/
